---
import ProductCaseStudyArticle from "../../../../components/ProductCaseStudyArticle.astro";
---

<ProductCaseStudyArticle
  slug="search-dead-ends"
  seoTitle="Why Customers Abandon a Search Before Seeing Results | Product Research Case Study"
  dek="Most funnel analysis starts at the results page. In many products, a meaningful share of demand is lost earlier, in the search box, where few teams are looking. This worked example shows how I find that leak, put a number on it and fix it."
  summary={[
    "If search does not recognise what people type, nothing else in the funnel matters.",
    "I split the problem into four hypotheses: the term is not recognised, it is recognised but leads nowhere, the same item exists twice, or finding it takes too much effort.",
    "Each hypothesis has a specific data check, so the diagnosis takes days, not months.",
    "A simple sizing model turns \"search could be better\" into a monthly revenue figure the business can rank against other work.",
    "Seven fixes follow, ordered by impact, confidence and effort, with a test plan for the first one.",
  ]}
  scenario={[
    "Imagine a booking site that sells ferry crossings and island transfers. Traffic to the home page is healthy, but the share of visitors who reach a results page is lower than the team expects, and nobody can say why. Analytics tracks page views and purchases, but not what happens inside the search box.",
    "Ferries make search hard. A port can be known by the town's name, the island's name, the pier's name or a name in another language. Some routes only run in summer. One island can have three piers, and the traveller rarely knows which one they need.",
  ]}
  hypotheses={[
    {
      name: "Not recognised.",
      claim: "Customers type names the site does not know.",
      signal: "Many searches with no suggestion; typos, local names and short forms in the failed queries",
      check: "Search logs and a new \"suggestion shown / empty / selected\" event",
    },
    {
      name: "Leads nowhere.",
      claim: "The place is suggested, but nothing can be booked.",
      signal: "Searches that return zero results, clustered on seasonal routes",
      check: "Join suggestions with availability for the searched date",
    },
    {
      name: "Split.",
      claim: "The same place exists twice.",
      signal: "Near-identical names or coordinates; results that look empty from one entry and full from another",
      check: "Catalogue audit by name similarity and distance",
    },
    {
      name: "Too much effort.",
      claim: "Finding the place takes too long on mobile.",
      signal: "Long typing time, repeated edits, drop-off before selecting",
      check: "Session recordings and time to first selection",
    },
  ]}
  evidence={[
    "Failed queries include island names in the local language, pier names without the town, and short forms that locals use.",
    "In winter, autocomplete still suggests summer-only ports. Customers search, see no departures and leave, concluding the site does not serve their route.",
    "Two entries exist for the same pier, each with part of the inventory. From one entry the route looks sold out.",
    "Recordings show customers typing the destination several times, then giving up.",
  ]}
  evidenceNote="Public research points the same way. Baymard's testing found that 69% of benchmarked e-commerce sites do not offer relevant autocomplete suggestions for closely misspelled queries, and that users who meet a brittle autocomplete often conclude the site does not have what they want."
  sizing={[
    { label: "Search sessions a month", value: "200,000" },
    { label: "Sessions that end with an empty suggestion list", value: "6% = 12,000" },
    { label: "Share a better search could recover", value: "One third = 4,000 sessions" },
    { label: "Results-to-booking rate", value: "10% = 400 bookings" },
    { label: "Average order", value: "€45" },
  ]}
  sizingResult="About €18,000 a month, from fixing what happens before the results page."
  sizingNote="The team can now argue about the inputs instead of opinions, and compare this with any other item on the roadmap."
  fixes={[
    {
      title: "Typo-tolerant search built from real failed queries",
      pattern: "Customers type names the catalogue does not hold.",
      change: "Map misspellings, local names, short forms and other languages to the right item, starting with the most frequent failed queries.",
      effect: "Fewer empty lists and more demand reaching the results page.",
    },
    {
      title: "Only suggest what can be booked",
      pattern: "Suggestions lead to routes with no service.",
      change: "Filter suggestions against what is available from the chosen origin, including seasonal timetables.",
      effect: "Fewer dead-end searches.",
    },
    {
      title: "Merge duplicates and fix locations",
      pattern: "The same place exists twice, with split inventory.",
      change: "Merge duplicates, correct names and coordinates, and fix which town or island each stop belongs to.",
      effect: "Complete results from a single, correct entry.",
    },
    {
      title: "Suggest a nearby alternative",
      pattern: "No exact match, but something close is available.",
      change: "Offer nearby departure or arrival points inside the search flow, with the distance.",
      effect: "Recover customers who would otherwise assume nothing exists.",
    },
    {
      title: "Offer the customer's location as the starting point",
      pattern: "Many customers start from where they are now.",
      change: "Offer \"Use my location\" or suggest the current town, without forcing it.",
      effect: "Less typing, especially on mobile and at the last minute.",
    },
    {
      title: "A simpler form for fixed routes",
      pattern: "Some products have only a few possible routes, such as port to town.",
      change: "Replace the open from/to form with a direction choice and a prefilled location.",
      effect: "Fewer fields and fewer wrong selections.",
    },
    {
      title: "Let customers choose the island, not the pier",
      pattern: "Customers do not know which pier they need, and the ticket may be valid at several.",
      change: "Group interchangeable stops into one bookable location, and show the pier after selection.",
      effect: "Simpler booking and fewer support questions.",
    },
  ]}
  priorities={[
    { cells: ["Only suggest what can be booked", "High", "High", "Low", "1"] },
    { cells: ["Typo-tolerant search", "High", "High", "Medium", "2"] },
    { cells: ["Merge duplicates", "Medium", "High", "Medium", "3"] },
    { cells: ["Simpler form for fixed routes", "Medium", "Medium", "Low", "4"] },
    { cells: ["Grouping, nearby alternatives, location", "Medium", "Medium", "Medium–High", "Next"] },
  ]}
  prioritiesNote="Fixes that remove a guaranteed dead end come first, because their impact is easy to prove and they are cheap to build."
  test={[
    { label: "Hypothesis", text: "Filtering suggestions to bookable destinations reduces zero-result searches and increases search-to-booking." },
    { label: "Primary metric", text: "Search-to-booking rate per search session." },
    { label: "Supporting metrics", text: "Empty-suggestion rate, zero-result rate and time to first selection." },
    { label: "Guardrail", text: "Support contacts about missing items, so hiding an option does not create a new problem." },
    { label: "Before testing", text: "Add the suggestion events. Without them, this leak stays invisible." },
  ]}
  elsewhere={[
    "The same leak appears in any product where people search for one specific thing. In e-commerce it is a product name typed with a typo or a brand's local spelling. In a marketplace it is a city, a service or a category the catalogue names differently. In SaaS it is a help-centre or settings search that returns nothing for the words customers actually use.",
    "The method does not change: log what people type, measure what returns nothing, map the gaps, and size the result before building anything.",
  ]}
  checklist={[
    "Type your 20 most searched items with a typo, a short form and a local or colloquial name.",
    "Export a month of failed queries and group them. The top groups are your synonym list.",
    "Check that every suggestion leads to at least one available result.",
    "List items with near-identical names that might be duplicates.",
    "Make sure suggestion outcomes are tracked as events.",
  ]}
  conclusion="Search quality looks like a data chore, but it is a revenue question. The work is a sequence: write the hypotheses, check each one with a specific data source, size the opportunity, fix the guaranteed dead ends first, then test. If you have never measured empty suggestions, that is where I would start."
  contactLine="Do people search on your site and leave without results? I can help you find out how much that costs and what to fix first."
  sources={[
    {
      name: "Baymard Institute — Offer relevant autocomplete suggestions for closely misspelled search terms",
      url: "https://baymard.com/blog/offer-autocomplete-suggestions-for-misspellings",
    },
    { name: "Baymard Institute — E-commerce search and autocomplete research", url: "https://baymard.com/ecommerce-search/articles" },
  ]}
/>
